lesson art-a-repeat-like-william-morris v=1 format=teach grade=4 unit=4 subject=art levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "A repeat like William Morris"
  goal "Tell a straight repeat from a half-drop, work out how many tiles a repeat needs, and design a tile whose edges are hidden."

  look {
    scene 30x11 {
      row all at=canvas(1, 1) space=2 align=start {
        tilerepeat straight drop=0 cols=4 rows=3 missing=-1 ring=-1 colour=green
        tilerepeat dropped drop=1 cols=4 rows=2 missing=-1 ring=-1 colour=green
      }
    }
    say """
      William Morris designed wallpapers from 1862. Each was printed from wooden blocks, one repeat
      after another down the paper. In a straight repeat every row lines up. In a half-drop, every
      other column starts half a tile lower, which hides the grid, so the eye follows the flowers
      instead of the edges.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice art.tile-count count=1 seed=1
    practice art.straight-or-half-drop count=2 seed=2
    level easy {
      worked art.tiles-to-cover w=12 h=12 level=medium
    }
    practice art.tiles-to-cover count=2 seed=1
    practice art.half-drop-count count=2 seed=3
    practice art.tiles-in-rows count=2 hard-count=1 seed=4
    level hard {
      practice art.half-drop-whole count=1 seed=2
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show art.half-drop-whole r=4 easy-r=3 hard-r=5
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show art.design-a-tile
  }
  remember {
    say "One tile, repeated. A half-drop hides the grid, and a shape that crosses the edge hides the join."
  }

  grown-ups """
    William Morris lived from 1834 to 1896 and his designs are long out of copyright, though the
    lesson uses a tile of our own. His papers were printed from hand-cut woodblocks, a block for
    each colour, with the pattern repeated down and across the roll, and wallpaper and fabric are
    still made from one repeat printed again and again. The questions are marked by the machine. The tile
    counts are multiplication and division met on a wall, and covering the wall takes two steps:
    how many tiles fit along, and how many rows fit up.

    The two-star question is about what a half-drop does at the edges of a sheet: the dropped
    columns have half a tile hanging over at the top and at the bottom, so one whole tile's worth
    is cut away. The design is the three-star question and is responded to rather than marked.
    In Morris's papers the stems and leaves run on across the joins of the repeat, and the child's
    tile is a success when a stranger cannot easily find where one tile ends.

    Respond by trying to find the edges together: "I can see where this tile ends, but here the leaf
    carries on into the next one." Ask what they would change to hide the last edge.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the wall has six tiles, straight or half-drop is as written, a wall of twelve by twelve is covered once in the teacher's pen before walls nine tall, the half-drop starts from three rows, the roll prints four rows, the two-star's sheet is three tiles tall, and the three-star is as written."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the wall has twelve tiles, straight or half-drop is as written, the walls to cover are fifteen or eighteen by twelve, the half-drop starts from five or six by four, the roll is four or five tiles wide and the second roll makes way for a second half-drop sheet, the two-star's sheet is five tiles tall, and the three-star is as written."
  }
}
